<h2 id="requirements">Uber and Lyft driver requirements in 2026</h2>
<p>Both companies screen drivers, and the rules vary by city and state. These are the general US requirements as of 2026; check your market's page before you apply.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Age:</strong> Uber says new drivers must be at least 23 and meet any higher local minimum. Lyft's minimum ranges from 21 to 25 by region, and many state pages list 25.</li>
  <li><strong>Driving history:</strong> Uber requires at least one year of licensed US driving experience, or three years if you are under 25. Lyft requires one year in several states.</li>
  <li><strong>License and documents:</strong> A valid US driver's license, proof of residency, a profile photo, and registration and insurance in your name.</li>
  <li><strong>Vehicle:</strong> An eligible four-door vehicle that passes inspection where required. Model-year limits vary by market (Lyft lists 2010 or newer in many states, with stricter rules in places like New York City). Salvage-title cars are generally not allowed.</li>
  <li><strong>Background check:</strong> Both run a driving record and criminal background screening, with periodic re-checks.</li>
</ul>
<p>No eligible car? Both apps partner with rental programs in many cities, but weekly rental costs can eat most of your earnings. Run the numbers carefully before committing.</p>

<h2 id="getting-started">How to start driving: step by step</h2>
<ol class="steps">
  <li><strong>Check your market's requirements.</strong> Age, vehicle year and any city licensing (some cities, like New York City, require a separate TLC license).</li>
  <li><strong>Call your insurer about a rideshare endorsement.</strong> Do this before your first trip, not after a fender-bender. See the insurance section below.</li>
  <li><strong>Apply to both Uber and Lyft.</strong> Upload your license, insurance and registration, and consent to the background check. Approval can take days to a few weeks.</li>
  <li><strong>Complete any vehicle inspection.</strong> Some markets require it; schedule it as soon as the app prompts you.</li>
  <li><strong>Set up your car.</strong> A sturdy phone mount, a fast charger and a dash cam are worth buying before your first shift.</li>
  <li><strong>Start a mileage log on day one.</strong> Use an app or a paper log. Every business mile is a deduction, and the IRS expects records.</li>
  <li><strong>Drive a few test shifts at different times.</strong> Compare your real earnings per hour and per mile, then build a schedule around the best windows.</li>
</ol>

[[gear:phone-mount,dash-cam,car-charger|Car setup for rideshare drivers]]

<h2 id="real-earnings">What drivers really earn after expenses</h2>
<p>Uber and Lyft show your gross earnings: fares plus tips plus bonuses. Your real income is what is left after the car costs that come with every mile. Pay varies widely by city, time and demand, so rather than quote a national average, here is how to do the math for your own shifts.</p>
<p>Take an example shift: 4 hours online, 80 miles driven (including the miles between rides), and $100 in gross earnings with tips. The IRS standard mileage rate is a reasonable estimate of what driving actually costs you per mile, including gas, maintenance, insurance and depreciation.</p>
<table class="data">
  <thead><tr><th>Example 4-hour shift</th><th>Amount</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Gross earnings (fares, tips, bonuses)</td><td>$100.00</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Car cost at 76¢/mile × 80 miles</td><td>−$60.80</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Net before tax</strong></td><td><strong>$39.20 (about $9.80/hr)</strong></td></tr>
    <tr><td>Gas only, at 25 mpg and $3.50/gal</td><td>−$11.20</td></tr>
    <tr><td><strong>Cash in pocket after gas</strong></td><td><strong>$88.80 (about $22/hr)</strong></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
<p>Both numbers are "true" in a sense. Gas is the cost you feel today; wear, tires and depreciation arrive later as repair bills and a lower trade-in value. The gap between them is why an efficient car matters so much: a hybrid getting 50 mpg costs far less per mile to run than an older SUV. Use the calculator below with your own miles, hours and fuel economy.</p>

[[tool:gig-calculator]]

<p>For a deeper breakdown by platform, use the <a href="/tools/gig-earnings-calculator/">gig earnings calculator</a>. The key metric to watch is earnings per mile, not per trip. Many experienced drivers skip requests that pay less than a set amount per mile, especially long pickups.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warn"><strong>Watch out:</strong> "Dead miles," the miles driving to a pickup or heading home empty, are unpaid but still cost you money. A trip that looks good on the screen can be a loser once you add a 15-minute pickup drive.</div>

<h2 id="insurance">The insurance gap and rideshare endorsements</h2>
<p>This is the part new drivers skip and later regret. Rideshare insurance works in phases:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>App off:</strong> Your personal policy covers you as normal.</li>
  <li><strong>App on, waiting for a request:</strong> Most personal policies exclude coverage while you are working. Uber and Lyft provide limited liability coverage in this phase, but generally nothing for damage to your own car.</li>
  <li><strong>En route to pickup and during the trip:</strong> Uber and Lyft's commercial policies apply, including contingent collision coverage if you carry it on your personal policy, subject to a deductible.</li>
</ul>
<p>A <strong>rideshare endorsement</strong> is an add-on to your personal policy that covers the waiting phase and can help with the platform deductible. Many major insurers offer one, often for a modest added premium. Not telling your insurer you drive rideshare can lead to a denied claim or a canceled policy. Coverage details vary by state and insurer, so ask for specifics in writing.</p>

<h2 id="best-times">Best times and places to drive</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Weekday commute hours:</strong> Roughly 6–9 a.m. and 4–7 p.m. bring steady demand in most cities, especially near business districts.</li>
  <li><strong>Friday and Saturday nights:</strong> Dinner rush, then the late-night window around bar close, often brings surge pricing. It also brings messier passengers, so seat covers and a dash cam earn their keep.</li>
  <li><strong>Airports:</strong> Long trips and good fares, but queues can be long. Check flight arrival banks and the app's queue length before you commit to waiting.</li>
  <li><strong>Concerts, games and conventions:</strong> Event endings create big, predictable surges. Position yourself a few blocks away from the venue to avoid gridlock.</li>
  <li><strong>Bad weather and holidays:</strong> Rain, snow, New Year's Eve and long weekends push demand up when many drivers stay home.</li>
</ul>
<p>Event-heavy cities reward this strategy most. See our city guides for <a href="/best-side-hustles-in-las-vegas/">Las Vegas</a>, <a href="/best-side-hustles-in-atlanta/">Atlanta</a>, <a href="/best-side-hustles-in-houston/">Houston</a>, <a href="/best-side-hustles-in-chicago/">Chicago</a> and <a href="/best-hustles-in-los-angeles/">Los Angeles</a> for local demand patterns and alternatives.</p>

<h2 id="multi-apping">Multi-apping with delivery apps</h2>
<p>Most full-time and serious part-time drivers do not rely on one app. Running Uber and Lyft together cuts your waiting time, and adding delivery fills the slow hours when ride demand drops:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Uber Eats:</strong> Built into the Uber Driver app, so you can accept both rides and deliveries.</li>
  <li><strong>DoorDash:</strong> Strong lunch and dinner volume in most markets; no passengers in your car.</li>
  <li><strong>Instacart:</strong> Grocery shopping and delivery with larger orders and often bigger tips, good for weekday daytime.</li>
</ul>
<p>The rule for multi-apping: when you accept a trip on one app, go offline on the others so you do not stack requests you cannot honor. An [[amazon:insulated-bag|insulated delivery bag]] keeps food orders hot and ratings high. More app-based ideas are in our guide to <a href="/side-hustles-you-can-start-with-just-your-smartphone/">side hustles you can start with just your smartphone</a>.</p>

<h2 id="earn-more">Seven ways to earn more per hour</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Track per-mile pay, not trip count.</strong> Decide your minimum acceptable dollars per mile and decline trips well below it, especially with long pickups.</li>
  <li><strong>Drive the peaks, skip the dead zones.</strong> Two hours at a Friday-night surge can beat five hours on a quiet Tuesday afternoon.</li>
  <li><strong>Stay near demand between trips.</strong> Rather than chasing a surge across town, park somewhere central and let requests come to you.</li>
  <li><strong>Earn tips with the basics.</strong> A clean car, a phone charger cable for passengers, a confirmed route and a friendly greeting do more than gimmicks.</li>
  <li><strong>Use the rewards programs.</strong> Uber Pro and Lyft's driver rewards tiers offer perks such as fuel and maintenance discounts. Check what your tier unlocks.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch promotions carefully.</strong> Quests and streak bonuses can add meaningfully to a week, but only chase them when the required trips fit your normal schedule.</li>
  <li><strong>Maintain your car on schedule.</strong> Oil changes, tires and brakes cost far less than a breakdown that takes you off the road for a week.</li>
</ol>

