<h2 id="taxes">Taxes for Uber and Lyft drivers</h2>
<p>Drivers are independent contractors, so neither app withholds taxes. You owe federal income tax plus 15.3% self-employment tax on your net profit. A few rules to follow:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Forms:</strong> Uber and Lyft provide annual tax summaries and issue 1099 forms when you meet the reporting thresholds. All earnings are taxable whether or not you receive a form.</li>
  <li><strong>Mileage deduction:</strong> For 2026, use 72.5 cents per mile for business miles driven January 1 through June 30 and 76 cents per mile from July 1 onward, per the <a href="https://www.irs.gov/tax-professionals/standard-mileage-rates">IRS</a>. That includes miles driving to pickups and between trips while working, but not your commute to the first ride. You can use actual expenses instead, but you cannot claim gas on top of the standard rate.</li>
  <li><strong>Other deductions:</strong> Phone and data plan (business share), tolls, parking while working, car washes, phone mounts and chargers.</li>
  <li><strong>Quarterly estimates:</strong> If you expect to owe $1,000 or more for the year, pay estimated taxes. For the 2026 tax year, payments are due April 15, June 15 and September 15, 2026, and January 15, 2027.</li>
</ul>
<p>The <a href="/tools/self-employment-tax-calculator/">self-employment tax calculator</a> shows how much to set aside from each weekly payout. A dedicated [[amazon:mileage-log|mileage log book]] is cheap insurance if the IRS ever asks for records.</p>

<h2 id="bottom-line">The bottom line</h2>
<p>Uber and Lyft are a legitimate way to earn quickly on your own schedule, but only if you measure the right number: earnings per hour and per mile after car costs. Drive an efficient car, target peak windows, multi-app to cut dead time, fix the insurance gap, and log every mile. Do that, and rideshare works as a flexible bridge or a steady supplement. Better Side Hustles recommends treating it as one tool in your kit rather than your only one.</p>
